","Marfan syndrome is caused by a mutation in the **FBN1 gene**. 

This gene provides instructions for making **fibrillin-1**, a protein that is a major component of **connective tissue**. Connective tissue is a type of tissue that provides support and structure to many parts of the body, including the heart, blood vessels, eyes, bones, skin, and lungs.

The mutations in the FBN1 gene disrupt the production of fibrillin-1, leading to weakened connective tissue. This weakness can cause a wide range of symptoms, depending on which parts of the body are affected.


It's important to note that Marfan syndrome is an **autosomal dominant disorder**, meaning that only one copy of the mutated gene is needed to cause the condition. 

